<Post title="Verification, CI, and the smart mesh bridge" part={5} date="2026-08-16" series="tlsr8266-firmware">
	<p class="tagline">
		Testing embedded firmware without physical hardware: mocking microcontroller MMIO on x86 with
		the <code>mry</code> crate, branch-coverage CI, and integrating our BLE mesh with home automation via a 44-byte UART gateway.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the testing challenge</h2>
	<p>
		Testing firmware on real microcontrollers is slow, difficult to automate, and prone to false
		positives. Flashing hardware over SWS or BLE takes time, and asserting state inside MCU registers
		requires physical probe setups.
	</p>
	<p>
		To make development rapid and testable, I designed the Rust codebase so that <strong>every driver,
		state machine, and protocol parser can be tested directly on an x86 Linux machine</strong>.
	</p>

	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		TEST["Host Unit Test<br/>(i686-unknown-linux-gnu)"] -->|"Configures mocks via mry"| MRY["mry Mocking Engine<br/>(Function-level interception)"]
		MRY <-->|"Intercepts read/write registers"| DRV["Rust Driver / Stack Under Test<br/>(register.rs)"]
		DRV -->|"Asserts expected output"| TEST`}</Mermaid>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>host MMIO register mocking with <code>mry</code></h2>
	<p>
		On real hardware, all peripherals are accessed via volatile pointer reads/writes at base
		address <code>0x800000</code>. To allow unit testing on x86 without hardware attached, I annotated
		every register accessor function in <code>rust/src/sdk/mcu/register.rs</code> with the <code>mry::mry</code> macro:
	</p>

	<pre><code>{`// In rust/src/sdk/mcu/register.rs:
pub const REG_BASE_ADDR: u32 = 0x800000;

#[coverage(off)]
#[cfg_attr(test, mry::mry)]
pub fn write_reg8(addr: u32, v: u8) {
    unsafe { core::ptr::write_volatile((addr | REG_BASE_ADDR) as *mut u8, v) }
}

#[coverage(off)]
#[cfg_attr(test, mry::mry)]
pub fn read_reg8(addr: u32) -> u8 {
    unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile((addr | REG_BASE_ADDR) as *mut u8) }
}`}</code></pre>

	<p>
		Under test builds (<code>#[cfg(test)]</code>), <code>mry</code> generates mock handles for each register function.
		Unit tests configure return values and assert that drivers interact with hardware registers correctly:
	</p>

	<pre><code>{`// In test suites: simulating hardware register responses
#[test]
fn test_rf_channel_configuration() {
    mock_read_reg8(0x40d).returns(0x02);
    mock_write_reg8(0x40d, 0x05).returns(());

    // Test driver logic against mocked register interactions
    assert_eq!(read_reg_rf_channel(), 0x02);
}`}</code></pre>

	<p>
		Because the TLSR8266 is a 32-bit architecture, tests are cross-compiled against <code>i686-unknown-linux-gnu</code>.
		This ensures identical pointer widths and struct layout rules, catching misaligned data access and
		endianness issues before code ever touches silicon.
	</p>
	<p>
		Running <code>./rust/run_tests.sh</code> executes <strong>975 unit tests</strong> covering BLE advertisement parsing,
		AES-CCM encryption, pairing transitions, and PWM calculations in under two seconds.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>continuous integration & quality gates</h2>
	<p>
		In <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>, the GitHub Actions pipeline enforces code quality on every pull request:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Formatting & Linting:</strong> Strict <code>cargo clippy --all-targets</code> and <code>rustfmt</code> verification.</li>
		<li><strong>Host Test Coverage:</strong> Live test reporting with branch-level metrics via <code>cargo-llvm-cov</code>.</li>
		<li><strong>Native LLVM Build:</strong> Builds the forked <code>llc</code>, <code>llvm-link</code>, and <code>opt</code> from source and verifies full firmware binary generation with zero warnings.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the smart mesh bridge (ditching the vendor brick)</h2>
	<p>
		The stock vendor controller was a cheap USB "Wi-Fi/BLE gateway brick". It dropped packets,
		lagged during mesh floods, and depended on vendor cloud endpoints.
	</p>
	<p>
		I replaced the vendor brick entirely with an <strong>E104-BT05-TB development board</strong> flashed
		with the custom Rust firmware, plugged directly into the home server as a dedicated serial bridge.
	</p>

	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		HA["Home Assistant / MQTT"] <-->|"JSON State"| SMART["Smart Manager Daemon<br/>(Rust service)"]
		SMART <-->|"115200 Baud 8N1<br/>44-byte Packets (CRC16)"| BRIDGE["E104-BT05-TB<br/>(Gateway Node)"]
		BRIDGE <-->|"2.4 GHz BLE GFSK<br/>AES-CCM Mesh Flood"| MESH["Mesh Light Nodes<br/>(TYBT1 Bulbs)"]`}</Mermaid>

	<h3>The 44-Byte Binary UART Protocol</h3>
	<p>
		The bridge communicates with the host <strong>Smart Manager Daemon</strong> (<code>smartmanager</code>)
		over serial at 115200 baud using structured 44-byte binary packets:
	</p>

	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Offset</th><th>Size</th><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>1</td><td>Sequence Counter</td><td>Incrementing transaction ID for matching acknowledgments</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>1</td><td>ACK Flag</td><td><code>0x00</code> for normal command; <code>0xFF</code> for acknowledgment</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>1</td><td>Command Type</td><td><code>0x02</code> (Light Ctrl), <code>0x03</code> (Status Req), <code>0x07</code> (Node Status)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>3..17</code></td><td>15</td><td>Payload Data</td><td>Target Node ID, brightness (<code>0x700-0x7FFF</code>), temperature, transition time</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>18</code></td><td>1</td><td>Reliability Flag</td><td><code>0x05</code> for retry with ACK confirmation; <code>0x00</code> for best-effort flood</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>19..41</code></td><td>23</td><td>Zero Padding</td><td>Reserved buffer padding</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>42..43</code></td><td>2</td><td>CRC16-ANSI</td><td>Little-endian CRC16 checksum calculated over bytes 0–41</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>
		Every few seconds during sunrise and sunset, the daemon calculates the next circadian target
		and sends a 44-byte command to the bridge. The mesh nodes receive the target and smoothly tween
		their PWM compare levels across 32,767 internal steps. No strobing, no step ladder, and no cloud dependency.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>retrospective & conclusions</h2>
	<p>
		What started as an annoyance with aggressive, non-tweening smart lights and a flaky gateway
		brick evolved into a solid, maintainable embedded stack:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Zero Proprietary Blobs:</strong> Liberation from closed vendor SDKs and cloud dependencies.</li>
		<li><strong>Native Toolchain:</strong> Upstream-tracked LLVM backend enabling standard Rust on obscure TC32 silicon.</li>
		<li><strong>Smooth Lighting:</strong> Sub-10ms response times, 32,767 resolution levels, and gradual circadian transitions.</li>
		<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> High branch coverage and host-side register simulation ensure predictable daily operation.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Every light in the house now runs code I wrote, compiled with a toolchain I maintain, speaking
		a protocol I understand end-to-end.
	</p>
